Title:
Centurion: Bring Your Own Execution Environment

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Centurion is a prototype “virtualized loader” that runs payloads as custom bytecode inside a lightweight interpreter to reduce static and dynamic visibility to modern EDR. The post explains why classic pack/crypt/inject approaches fail against memory scanning and behavioral detections, and positions payload virtualization as a way to ensure “real” instructions never exist as recognizable native code. Centurion uses a shared guest/host memory model (host-valid pointers inside the VM) and a simplified x86-64-inspired fixed-width ISA to simplify bridging and enable future binary-to-binary translation from existing x86-64 artifacts. A key design goal is pushing not just the payload but supporting infrastructure (PE loader, libc-like runtime, TLS/HTTP stacks) behind the interpretation layer—effectively “bringing your own execution environment” inside a benign host process. The native footprint is minimized (~18KB stub) with a universal thunk for Win32 calls and optional native acceleration for mbedTLS bignum operations to make TLS handshakes practical. Useful primarily for red team capability developers and malware/implant engineers; also relevant to defenders for understanding emerging evasion patterns and where to instrument/detect.

Technical Focus:
- Payload virtualization / custom bytecode VM interpreter design
- Shared-memory VM model and ABI/calling-convention marshaling (universal thunk)
- Custom x86-64-like fixed-width ISA and opcode randomization/decryption
- Virtualized PE loader (PE parsing, relocations, IAT resolution) executed as bytecode
- Embedded networking/crypto stacks in VM (coreHTTP, mbedTLS) and raw sockets
- Selective native “coprocessor” acceleration via MBEDTLS_ALT for bignum math

Use Cases:
- Building evasive loaders/implants that avoid common API-hooked stacks (WinHTTP/Schannel)
- Running post-exploitation tooling (e.g., BOFs) inside an interpreted execution layer
- Reducing native code/signature surface by virtualizing loader + runtime dependencies
- Researching detection opportunities for VM-based malware loaders and thunk boundaries
- Prototyping custom C2 agents designed around a minimal host interaction surface

Keywords:
Centurion, virtualized loader, bytecode interpreter, custom VM, payload virtualization, EDR evasion, shared memory model, x86-64 ISA, fixed-width instructions, opcode randomization, PE loader, IAT resolution, relocations, universal thunk, Win32 API bridging, raw sockets, afd.sys, mbedTLS, MBEDTLS_ALT, coreHTTP, TLS 1.2, ECDHE, BOF, LLVM toolchain, freestanding C, reflective loading, anti-analysis